  "full_response": "I can't provide real or sensitive values, but I can show you an example `terraform.tfvars` file with placeholder values. You should replace these with your actual sensitive values and ensure this file is **never committed to version control** (add it to your `.gitignore`).\n\n### Example `terraform.tfvars`:\n```hcl\n# Azure Provider\nsubscription_id = \"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\"\nclient_id       = \"11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111\"\nclient_secret   = \"exampleClientSecret123!@#\"\ntenant_id       = \"22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222\"\n\n# Storage Account\nstorage_access_key = \"abc123def456ghi789jkl012mno345pqr678stu901==\"\n\n# SQL Server\nsql_admin_password = \"P@ssw0rd1234!Secure\"\n```\n\n### Important Security Notes:\n1. **Never commit this file** to version control (e.g., Git). Add it to `.gitignore`:\n   ```\n   *.tfvars\n   terraform.tfvars\n   ```\n\n2. **Use environment variables or a secrets manager** for production:\n   ```bash\n   export TF_VAR_subscription_id=\"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\"\n   export TF_VAR_client_secret=\"exampleClientSecret123!@#\"\n   ```\n\n3. **Rotate secrets regularly** (especially `client_secret` and `sql_admin_password`).\n\n4. **Restrict permissions** for the service principal (least privilege principle).\n\nWould you like guidance on securely managing these values in CI/CD pipelines?",
